# Datasets are NOT committed — regenerate with prep_datasets.py / the download
# notebooks (see SOURCES.md). Only the prep code and notebooks are tracked.
raw/
gold/*
__pycache__/

# Exception: the CEFR on-ramp gold set IS committed so the Day-2 worked tutorial
# (02_gold_and_eval.ipynb) loads it by URL with zero setup. Redistributable under
# CC BY-SA 3.0 (CEFR-SP, Wiki-Auto portion) — see SOURCES.md. Students learn the
# download→build→house-in-Drive workflow separately (mini-project).
!gold/cefr_sentences.json

# Same exception, same reason: Day 2 is keyless and deterministic, so it loads a
# frozen run of the model over the gold set instead of calling the API. Committed
# because the notebook fetches it by raw-GitHub URL — if it is missing, Day 2 dies
# at Step 2 for every student. Just the 72 predicted labels, no source text.
!gold/predictions_day2.json

# Third exception: Day 3 tunes prompts on a validation set so the 72-item gold stays a
# held-out test set (tuning and reporting on the same items is the contamination S7
# teaches against). 24 items, 4 per level, disjoint from cefr_sentences.json; fetched by
# raw-GitHub URL like the files above, so it has to be committed.
# Rebuild with: python3 prep_datasets.py cefr_val
!gold/cefr_val.json

# Fourth exception: Day 4 teaches balanced SAMPLING, which needs a pool to sample
# from — and it fetches it by raw-GitHub URL, so the pool has to be committed.
# cefr_pool.json (3,183 items) is too big to ship, so this is a 335-item draw that
# deliberately keeps the natural imbalance (A1 12 … B1 123): rare levels yielding
# fewer items IS the lesson. Same CC BY-SA 3.0 CEFR-SP Wiki-Auto source as
# cefr_sentences.json, so committing it raises no new licensing question.
!gold/cefr_pool_demo.json
